      "writtenOn": "2025-09-11T09:49:24Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "The macro injects data into `.o` files for `gendwarfksyms` to find. You could put the same macro invocation in the 10s of files that include the header or in the 1 header file. It will make no difference to the final result.\n\nObviously, the current solution of touching 1 file is better than touching a lot of files, but it compromises UAPI.\n\n\u003e Would that only fix the issue for `tools/iio`?\n\nAsk a kernel developer? My guess of the intent of the `ln` in the `Makefile` is to ensure that UAPI and internal headers are the same.\n\nBut then there\u0027s the bizarre line in `include/linux/iio/types.h` where it tries to include the other copy of itself:\n\n```\n#include \u003cuapi/linux/iio/types.h\u003e\n```\n\nSo, I may have misunderstood the relationship between the two `iio/types.h` files.\n\nThe UAPI one defines `iio_chan_type`, `iio_modifier`, `iio_event_type` and `iio_event_direction`.\n\nThe internal one defines `iio_event_info`, `iio_available_type`, `iio_chan_info_enum`.\n\nThe internal one includes the external one. No other code does.\n\nI don\u0027t actually know what that `ln` is doing. It almost looks overwriting the internal one with the external one, but that cannot be right. Right?! (Please confirm.)\n\nIf the `ln` is a red herring, then all you should have to do is move the macro stuff to the internal `types.h`.",
      "parentUuid": "87eb8695_71987b45",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 56,
        "startChar": 0,
        "endLine": 61,
        "endChar": 6
